# Reference Links

Numbered reference-link definitions used by the tuning docs. Each definition keeps its number so existing `[n]` citations still resolve. Two URLs were replaced on 2026-11-07: [16] (moved) and [41] (was about SQL Server). The table below records the status and reliability of each link, checked 2026-11-07.

**Status:** OK (loads, HTTP 200), Redirect (moves to a new URL), Blocked (refuses automated clients, content not verified), Dead, Paywalled.
**Type:** primary (PostgreSQL project or AWS product docs), vendor (blog of a company selling PostgreSQL products or services), blog (individual author), forum (Q&A or discussion).

**Notes:**
- Links to `postgresql.org/docs/current/` follow the newest release. They showed PostgreSQL 18 on 2026-11-07 and will move to PostgreSQL 19 when it is released (planned 2026-11-19). For version-specific claims, cite a pinned URL such as `/docs/14/` or `/docs/18/`.
- Duplicates: [17] is the same URL as [5], [18] as [8], [19] as [9], and [27] as [6]. Prefer the lower number in new text.
- Numbers 7, 22 to 26, 28, 30 and 51 to 61, 63 to 75 are not defined here.
- Secondary sources (vendor, blog, forum) are useful for explanation, but check their numbers against the manual. Several of them give fixed values (for example `effective_io_concurrency = 200` or `random_page_cost = 1.1`) that should be treated as starting points to test.

